{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-83897" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The growth rate of the hedgehog population $P(t)$ in a certain forest is jointly proportional to the hedgehog population at time $t,$ measured in years, and the difference between the forest's carrying capacity and the hedgehog population. The constant of proportionality is $0.000\\,005$ and the carrying capacity is $50\\,000.$ How many hedgehogs will there be in the forest after $20$ years given that there were $12\\,500$ hedgehogs initially? Round your answer to the nearest integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071481/state.json" "file_sha256" "035c3973f154583cda63f99b87d0d9d0e1cf81062f646b53f6a51dcc8f3ab68f"} {"question" "q-83897" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the hedgehog population's growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the hedgehog population at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the hedgehog population, the hedgehog population follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.000\\,005,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=50\\,000.$ Therefore, the hedgehog population growth is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,005P(50\\,000-P).\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{50\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-(50\\,000\\cdot 0.000\\,005)t}}=\\frac{50\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-0.25t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=12\\,500$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =12\\,500 \\\\ \\frac{50\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-0.25(0)}} & =12\\,500 \\\\ 1+C & =4 \\\\ C & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{50\\,000}{1+3{e}^{-0.25\\cdot t}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $t=20$ into the expression for $P(t),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(20) & =\\frac{50\\,000}{1+3{e}^{-0.25\\cdot 20}} \\\\  & ≈49\\,009.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071481/state.json" "file_sha256" "035c3973f154583cda63f99b87d0d9d0e1cf81062f646b53f6a51dcc8f3ab68f"} {"question" "q-83897" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071481/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "991338e5098117ffd616756a317f66295279643efc0207353bb1b9e092bcca46"} {"question" "q-83897" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071481/state.json" "file_sha256" "035c3973f154583cda63f99b87d0d9d0e1cf81062f646b53f6a51dcc8f3ab68f"} {"question" "q-83897" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "29\\,991"] ["math" "31\\,284"] ["math" "37\\,993"] ["math" "48\\,701"] ["math" "49\\,009"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071481/state.json" "file_sha256" "035c3973f154583cda63f99b87d0d9d0e1cf81062f646b53f6a51dcc8f3ab68f"} {"question" "q-83897" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "49\\,009"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071481/state.json" "file_sha256" "035c3973f154583cda63f99b87d0d9d0e1cf81062f646b53f6a51dcc8f3ab68f"} {"question" "q-84172" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The growth rate of the hamster population $P(t)$ in the Pets' Corner pet shop is jointly proportional to the hamster population at time $t,$ measured in years, and the difference between Pets' Corner's carrying capacity and the hamster population. If the constant of proportionality is $0.002\\,5$ and the carrying capacity is $100,$ then find an expression for the hamster population after $t$ years, given that there were $5$ hamsters in the pets' corner initially." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071481/state.json" "file_sha256" "035c3973f154583cda63f99b87d0d9d0e1cf81062f646b53f6a51dcc8f3ab68f"} {"question" "q-84172" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the hamster population's growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the hamster population at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the hamster population, the hamster population follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality $k=0.002\\,5,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=100.$ Therefore, the hamster population growth is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.002\\,5P(100-P).\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{100}{1+C{e}^{-(100\\cdot 0.002\\,5)t}}=\\frac{100}{1+C{e}^{-0.25t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=5$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{100}{1+C{e}^{-0.25\\cdot 0}} & =5 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{100}{5} \\\\ C & =20-1 \\\\  & =19.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{100}{1+19{e}^{-0.25t}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071481/state.json" "file_sha256" "035c3973f154583cda63f99b87d0d9d0e1cf81062f646b53f6a51dcc8f3ab68f"} {"question" "q-84172" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071481/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "991338e5098117ffd616756a317f66295279643efc0207353bb1b9e092bcca46"} {"question" "q-84172" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071481/state.json" "file_sha256" "035c3973f154583cda63f99b87d0d9d0e1cf81062f646b53f6a51dcc8f3ab68f"} {"question" "q-84172" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "P(t)=\\frac{100}{1+19{e}^{-0.25t}}"] ["math" "P(t)=\\frac{100}{1+19{e}^{-5t}}"] ["math" "P(t)=\\frac{100}{1+5{e}^{-0.25t}}"] ["math" "P(t)=\\frac{1\\,000}{1+199{e}^{-0.25t}}"] ["math" "P(t)=\\frac{1\\,000}{1+5{e}^{-2.5t}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071481/state.json" "file_sha256" "035c3973f154583cda63f99b87d0d9d0e1cf81062f646b53f6a51dcc8f3ab68f"} {"question" "q-84172" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "P(t)=\\frac{100}{1+19{e}^{-0.25t}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071481/state.json" "file_sha256" "035c3973f154583cda63f99b87d0d9d0e1cf81062f646b53f6a51dcc8f3ab68f"} {"question" "q-119862" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The growth rate of the flamingo population $P(t)$ in in a certain lagoon is jointly proportional to the flamingo population at time $t,$ measured in years, and the difference between the lagoon's carrying capacity and the flamingo population. If the constant of proportionality is $0.000\\,8$ and the carrying capacity is $16\\,000,$ then find an expression for the flamingo population after $t$ years, given that there were $2\\,000$ flamingos in the lagoon initially." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071481/state.json" "file_sha256" "035c3973f154583cda63f99b87d0d9d0e1cf81062f646b53f6a51dcc8f3ab68f"} {"question" "q-119862" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the flamingo population's growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the flamingo population at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the flamingo population, the flamingo population follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.000\\,8,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=16\\,000.$ Therefore, the flamingo population growth is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,8P(16\\,000-P).\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{16\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-(16\\,000\\cdot 0.000\\,8)t}}=\\frac{16\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-12.8t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=2\\,000$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{16\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-12.8\\cdot 0}} & =2\\,000 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{16\\,000}{2\\,000} \\\\ C & =8-1 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{16\\,000}{1+7{e}^{-12.8t}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071481/state.json" "file_sha256" "035c3973f154583cda63f99b87d0d9d0e1cf81062f646b53f6a51dcc8f3ab68f"} {"question" "q-119862" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071481/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "991338e5098117ffd616756a317f66295279643efc0207353bb1b9e092bcca46"} {"question" "q-119862" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071481/state.json" "file_sha256" "035c3973f154583cda63f99b87d0d9d0e1cf81062f646b53f6a51dcc8f3ab68f"} {"question" "q-119862" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "P(t)=\\frac{16\\,000}{1+7{e}^{-0.0008t}}"] ["math" "P(t)=\\frac{16\\,000}{1+7{e}^{-12.8t}}"] ["math" "P(t)=\\frac{2\\,000}{1+7{e}^{-0.0008t}}"] ["math" "P(t)=\\frac{2\\,000}{1+7{e}^{-12.8t}}"] ["math" "P(t)=\\frac{7}{1+{e}^{-t}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071481/state.json" "file_sha256" "035c3973f154583cda63f99b87d0d9d0e1cf81062f646b53f6a51dcc8f3ab68f"} {"question" "q-119862" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "P(t)=\\frac{16\\,000}{1+7{e}^{-12.8t}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071481/state.json" "file_sha256" "035c3973f154583cda63f99b87d0d9d0e1cf81062f646b53f6a51dcc8f3ab68f"} {"question" "q-353260" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The population $P(t)$ of insects in a greenhouse follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.6P-0.002{P}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, measured in days. If there were $40$ insects in the greenhouse initially, how many insects were in the greenhouse after $3$ days? Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\nAfter $3$ days, approximately\n{{field-1}} insects were in the greenhouse." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071481/state.json" "file_sha256" "035c3973f154583cda63f99b87d0d9d0e1cf81062f646b53f6a51dcc8f3ab68f"} {"question" "q-353260" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}},\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe start by factoring the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{dt} & =0.6P-0.002{P}^{2} \\\\  & =0.002P(300-P).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the constant of proportionality is $k=0.002,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=300.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{300}{1+C{e}^{-(300\\cdot 0.002)t}}=\\frac{300}{1+C{e}^{-0.6t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=40$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{300}{1+C{e}^{-0.6(0)}} & =40 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{300}{40} \\\\ C & =7.5-1 \\\\  & =6.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{300}{1+6.5{e}^{-0.6t}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $t=3$ into the expression for $P(t),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3) & =\\frac{300}{1+6.5{e}^{-0.6(3)}} \\\\  & ≈145.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $3$ days, approximately $145$ insects were in the greenhouse." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071481/state.json" "file_sha256" "035c3973f154583cda63f99b87d0d9d0e1cf81062f646b53f6a51dcc8f3ab68f"} {"question" "q-353260" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071481/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "991338e5098117ffd616756a317f66295279643efc0207353bb1b9e092bcca46"} {"question" "q-353260" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071481/state.json" "file_sha256" "035c3973f154583cda63f99b87d0d9d0e1cf81062f646b53f6a51dcc8f3ab68f"} {"question" "q-353260" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "145"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071481/state.json" "file_sha256" "035c3973f154583cda63f99b87d0d9d0e1cf81062f646b53f6a51dcc8f3ab68f"} {"question" "q-353263" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The population $P(t)$ of customers using a new ride-share service in a city follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.18P-0.000\\,12{P}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, measured in months. If there were $900$ customers using the service initially, how many customers were using the service after $8$ months? Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\nAfter $8$ months, approximately\n{{field-1}} customers were using the service." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071481/state.json" "file_sha256" "035c3973f154583cda63f99b87d0d9d0e1cf81062f646b53f6a51dcc8f3ab68f"} {"question" "q-353263" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}},\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe start by factoring the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{dt} & =0.18P-0.000\\,12{P}^{2} \\\\  & =0.000\\,12P(1500-P).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the constant of proportionality is $k=0.00012,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=1500.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{1500}{1+C{e}^{-(1500\\cdot 0.000\\,12)t}}=\\frac{1500}{1+C{e}^{-0.18t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=900$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1500}{1+C{e}^{-0.18(0)}} & =900 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{1500}{900} \\\\ C & =\\frac{5}{3}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{1500}{1+\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{-0.18t}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $t=8$ into the expression for $P(t),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(8) & =\\frac{1500}{1+\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{-0.18(8)}} \\\\  & ≈1295.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $8$ months, approximately $1295$ customers were using the service." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071481/state.json" "file_sha256" "035c3973f154583cda63f99b87d0d9d0e1cf81062f646b53f6a51dcc8f3ab68f"} {"question" "q-353263" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071481/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "991338e5098117ffd616756a317f66295279643efc0207353bb1b9e092bcca46"} {"question" "q-353263" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071481/state.json" "file_sha256" "035c3973f154583cda63f99b87d0d9d0e1cf81062f646b53f6a51dcc8f3ab68f"} {"question" "q-353263" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1295"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071481/state.json" "file_sha256" "035c3973f154583cda63f99b87d0d9d0e1cf81062f646b53f6a51dcc8f3ab68f"}] "usage" {} "basis" 2556 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
